 of  the expansion coefficients between the  two series and such a shift becomes  irrelevant in the limit of q tending to  infinity  overall this means that both series have  the same limit for large q  and in turn this means that if our  serious expansion for v is infinite then  the v function behaves like this  exponential for higher order terms in  the expansion  and this is where we have a problem this  exponential has a positive argument here  so it becomes increasingly large for  large role  this means that this v is a physically  unacceptable solution because the  resulting wave function would not be  normalizable  and therefore the key question becomes  how can we avoid this unphysical  solution for v  and of course the answer is that  physically relevant solutions correspond  to past series with a finite number of  terms  before we explore what that means a  quick aside having an infinite series is  a mathematically valid solution that  leads to the physically invalid solution  we already found when we solved the  differential equation in the limit of  large r  but now it is appearing for the full  equation  so we need to ensure that this power  series is finite  to do so all we need is to find an  expansion coefficient term say the kth  expansion coefficient  such that c k vanishes  given the recurrence relation up here  this immediately implies that all higher  expansion coefficients also vanish  and the series becomes finite as  required  so the question now is how can we ensure  that ck vanishes  this will happen if the numerator up  here is equal to zero when q equals k  explicitly the series will terminate if  lambda times k plus l minus one  is equal to zero  rearranging we get that lambda is equal  to one over k plus l  at this point it is important to note  that i've conveniently labeled by k this  integer index at which the series  terminates  this is because this is a number we can  use to label the different eigenvalues  for a given l and we had already decided  to call this quantum number k so i am  now using this label  finally  remember from earlier in the video that  we defined lambda kl as equal to the  square root of minus the energy  eigenvalues ekl divided by the  ionization energy e i  rearranging we get that the energy  eigenvalues are equal to minus ei times  lambda squared  and using the result we just found for  lambda up here and inserting it in this  expression we get minus e i over k plus  l squared  also remember that l is a non-negative  integer and as c zero must be different  from zero as shown up here this means  that k must be strictly larger than zero  and  this implies that the allowed values of  k  are positive integers  so  by insisting that the series must be  finite  we have now come to a key result  the energy eigenvalues of the bound  states of the hydrogen atom are  quantized and the energy levels are  given by this expression  now this is a hugely important result  but we won't explore it any further  today as we're only focusing on the  maths however you can check out the  videos that we have linked in the  description and they have a lot more  information and discussion on these  energy eigenvalues  we're now ready to go back to our  recurrence relation and determine the  values of the expansion coefficients in  terms of the first coefficient c0  to do so we're first going to simplify  the expression we have for the  recurrence relation by inserting this  value of lambda  inside this expression  doing so we get the ratio of cq to cq  minus 1 as equal to this numerator where  i am already inserting the value we have  for lambda  over this denominator  if we now focus on this term in the  numerator we can copy it down  we can then combine the two terms to get  this  we can now cancel the l here with the  one here and end up with this expression  going back to the recurrence relation at  the top we can rewrite it like this  where i am doing some trivial  rearrangement  and this is the simple recurrence  relation that we will use moving forward  let's start by copying the recurrence  relation where i am moving the c q minus  1 factor to the right as it will prove  convenient in a moment  next we want to use the recurrence  relation again but now write c q minus 1  as equal to the corresponding fraction  times c q minus two  do you pause here to double check this  step all we've done is to replace q by q  minus one  inserting this expression inside the one  above we first get the q independent  terms which now appear to the power 2  and then this long expression with the q  dependent terms  times c q minus 2.  this now gives us a relation between c q  here and c q minus 2 here  and we now play the same game again and  again until we arrive at c q  equal to the power q of the q  independent terms  then this very long expression involving  the q dependent terms  and all multiplying c0  and again feel free to pause here to  make sure of this latest expression  which may require you writing out a few  more terms explicitly before it clicks  the final step is to first pick this  term in the numerator here which we can  rewrite to this expression in terms of a  ratio of factorials this term in the  denominator which is simply q factorial  and this other term in the denominator  which again we can rewrite in terms of  another ratio of factorials  this allows us to write the final  expression for c q as equal to minus 1  to the power q multiplying 2 over k plus  l all to the power q  multiplying these two fractions  involving factorials  and all multiplying c0  and finally we have a general expansion  coefficient cq in terms of c0  and that's it  after this incredibly long and  mathematical derivation we now have all  the ingredients that we need to study  the energy eigenvalues here and  eigenfunctions here of the bound states  of the hydrogen atom  so we'll now finish with a brief summary  of the key results which we then use in  other videos to explore from a more  physical perspective the meaning of the  eigenvalues and eigenfunctions  so the full eigenfunction psi is equal  to the product of a radial part and an  angular part  the angular part is given by the  spherical harmonics because the  hamiltonian of the hydrogen atom  commutes with the orbital angular  momentum operators  the radial part is the trickier part to  determine and we've done just that in  this video by solving a rather complex  differential equation  doing so has required a large number of  steps  first we've rewritten the radial  function in terms of a new function u  like this  we've then made a change of variables to  rewrite u  as a function of a new variable rho  where rho is the ratio of r and the  reduced bohr radius  then by considering the limit of large  rho we've defined yet another function v  which is related to the u function via  this relation and where lambda is  defined as equal to the square root of  minus the eigenvalue over the ionization  energy  next we've expressed the function v in a  power series like this where the  exponent s is equal to l plus one  and finally we've shown that the  expansion coefficient cq is related to  the expansion coefficient c0 by this  rather long expression  we can then complete the solution by  fixing c0 through normalizing the wave  function  and this is it this is all we need to  determine the eigenfunctions once we've  determined these c expansion  coefficients we can construct v once we  have v we can construct u in terms of  the row variable we can then go back to  the original coordinate r  and once we have u like this we can  construct the radial function and once  we have r we can finally build the  eigenfunctions  with this recipe we can now build any  eigenfunction we want and you can find  examples of this in the videos linked in  the description  okay so if you've made it this far  know that this is our longest video so  far so thank you for sticking around i  encourage you to check out the rest of  the videos on the hydrogen atom series  to gain a more conceptual understanding  of the mathematical solution that we've  derived today and as always if you liked  the video please subscribe
 

